<%# console.about — about.php. DEV-009: about.php is a release announcement for WordPress
    the PROJECT ("Welcome to WordPress 7.1", the feature showcase, the release notes and
    field-guide links, the s.w.org imagery). None of that is true of this system, so none
    of it is migrated. What IS carried across is the screen's INFORMATION ARCHITECTURE —
    title, secondary tabs, a lede, a run of feature blocks, a "learn more" pair and the
    return-to-dashboard link — refilled with statements about THIS build.

    ⚠️ Every claim below is checkable: the runtime row is read from the running process and
    the live connection (InfoController#runtime_facts), and each deviation is cited by its
    id in _reversa_sdd/migration/screen_deviation_log.md. Nothing here is decorative. -%>
<% @page_title = "About" %>
<h1 class="wp-heading-inline">About</h1>
<hr class="wp-header-end">

<%= render "console/info/nav" %>

<div class="about-section">
  <h2>About this build</h2>
  <p class="about-lede">This administration console belongs to a Rails rebuild of a
  WordPress 7.2-alpha site, reverse-engineered screen by screen under the Reversa
  framework. The public site is held to byte-for-byte parity against captured golden
  files. The console is not: it is rebuilt rather than copied, so its structure is this
  build&#8217;s own, while every functional label, prompt, validation message and error
  string is carried across verbatim from the legacy source.</p>
</div>

<%# about.php's run of feature blocks. Ours describe what actually CHANGED against the
    legacy — the four architectural decisions a person using this console will notice. -%>
<div class="about-columns">
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>No plugins, no hooks</h3>
    <p><strong>The extension mechanism is gone, on purpose.</strong></p>
    <p>The legacy&#8217;s hook and filter system is what the entire plugin ecosystem
    attached to. This build does not have one, and with nothing for a plugin to attach to
    the plugin-management screens are not built. Themes stay: a theme is data and template
    files, not code hooking into the core.</p>
    <p class="description">AD-01; DEV-011.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Authorization is declared at the route</h3>
    <p><strong>Every route states who may reach it, or the application does not
    boot.</strong></p>
    <p>In the legacy, a capability check is a line inside a page script; omit it and the
    page is simply open. Here each route carries an explicit authorization declaration and
    a missing one is a startup failure, not a silent hole. A refusal still answers with
    the legacy&#8217;s own wording.</p>
    <p class="description">AD-04.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>The menu is declared, not registered</h3>
    <p><strong>The list of screens is data this build owns.</strong></p>
    <p>wp-admin assembles its menu by letting code register into it while the request
    runs. Without a hook system the navigation is a declared structure instead: one entry
    per screen that exists, each naming the capability that reveals it, resolved before
    the page is drawn.</p>
    <p class="description">DEV-002.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Destructive bulk actions confirm first</h3>
    <p><strong>One extra step before anything is deleted in bulk.</strong></p>
    <p>A bulk action that destroys rows shows what it is about to do and waits for a
    second submit. The outcome is the legacy&#8217;s; the extra step is not.</p>
    <p class="description">DEV-004.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="about-feature">
  <h2>Why this page does not look like the legacy one</h2>
  <p>In the legacy, About, Credits and Freedoms described WordPress the project: its
  release, its contributors, its trademark policy. Reproducing that text here would be a
  false claim about what this system is, so it is not migrated. Project-identity strings
  are the single category of text this rebuild is allowed to drop — every functional
  string on every other screen is still verbatim.</p>
  <p class="description">DEV-009.</p>
</div>

<%# about.php prints "WordPress %s" from a constant. The honest equivalent is not a
    constant at all: it is what this process and this connection actually report. -%>
<div class="about-section">
  <h2>What is running</h2>
  <table class="about-facts">
    <tbody>
      <% @runtime.each do |label, value| %>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row"><%= label %></th>
          <td><%= value %></td>
        </tr>
      <% end %>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p class="description">Read from the running process and the live database connection
  when this page was rendered, not from a version written into the page.</p>
</div>

<%# about.php's closing pair of cards (Release Notes / Field Guide) pointed at
    wordpress.org. The equivalent here is internal: where this build's own record lives. -%>
<div class="about-columns">
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Specifications and business rules</h3>
    <p>The screens, rules and data model this build was written against are kept in the
    repository under <code>_reversa_sdd/</code>. A screen&#8217;s behaviour is settled
    there before it is settled here.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="panel">
    <h3>Deviation log</h3>
    <p>Every departure from legacy behaviour — including the ones named on this page — is
    recorded with its reason and its approval in
    <code>_reversa_sdd/migration/screen_deviation_log.md</code>.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<% if @can_view_site_health %>
  <div class="about-section">
    <p><%= link_to "See the full status report", "/console/tools/site-health/info" %> for the
    environment detail behind the table above.</p>
  </div>
<% end %>

<%# about.php:406-412 — the return link, LITERAL. -%>
<div class="about-return">
  <a href="/console">Go to Dashboard &rarr; Home</a>
</div>
